<div style="background-color: #e0f2f7; padding: 20px; border-radius: 8px; text-align: center; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0; padding-bottom: 5px;">Lesson Planner – Theme # 1 – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="color: #333; margin: 0;"><em>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</em></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> Pre-K (CEFR: A1.1)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Meeting My Friends</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Saying Please and Thank You</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Scribble to communicate a message. Interactive: Draw a symbol or image as a response to something.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can scribble to represent an idea (e.g., scribbling to show friends saying "hi" to each other). Can draw an image to express a response to their peers (e.g., saying "hello" while showing their drawing). Can draw a simple picture representing themselves or a friend using greetings or polite expressions (e.g., drawing themselves saying "hello" to a new friend; drawing one child saying "please" and another saying "thank you" while sharing toys).</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center; margin-top: 30px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f0f8ff;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; width: 34%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher leads a "Hello Friends" song with simple TPR gestures, focusing on waving and saying "hello." The teacher then uses realia (e.g., a toy car) to model "please" and "thank you" in a simple exchange: "Please, give me the car." (Receives car) "Thank you!" (Gives car back) "Please, take the car."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models drawing a simple happy face and then scribbling next to it while saying "hello!" The teacher then models drawing two simple stick figures waving at each other, saying "Hello, friend!" The teacher explicitly models the pronunciation of "friend" with clear syllable stress and the initial /f/ sound. Teacher also models saying "please" and "thank you" while showing corresponding actions (asking, giving).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher points to a drawing of a child waving) "Is this 'hello'?" (Students nod or say "Yes!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Teacher points to a drawing of a child giving a toy) "Is this 'thank you'?" (Students give a thumbs up or say "Yes!")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Meeting My Friends."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Large, colorful illustrated story cards or a digital presentation showing a simple "Social Story: Meeting My Friends." The story features two friends, Leo and Mia. The cards depict them meeting ("hello"), playing together and sharing a toy (one asks "please," the other says "thank you"), and then saying "goodbye" (pronouncing /g/ in goodbye). Each card has a visual cue (e.g., a speech bubble with a wave symbol for "hello," or a hand symbol for "please/thank you").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Expressing basic greetings and polite expressions through simple written forms (scribbles, drawings).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks (for Pre-K):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher shows cards: "Who is the friend?" (Students point to Leo or Mia).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher points to the "hello" card: "What are they saying?" (Students say "hello" or wave).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher points to the "thank you" card: "What polite word do they use?" (Students may say "thank you" or mimic the action).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">7-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Writing (scribbling/drawing to represent ideas).</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice representing greetings and polite expressions (hello, goodbye, please, thank you) through simple drawings and scribbles. They engage with the concepts of "friend" and basic social interactions. Target grammar: Subject + Verb concepts (e.g., "I am [name]" represented by drawing self), WH-questions and Yes/No Questions (teacher prompts for clarification). Vocabulary: friend, name, please, thank you, hello, goodbye.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice: Polite Symbol Drawing.</b> The teacher distributes worksheets with two large, empty "speech bubbles" next to an outline of a child. The teacher says, "Let's draw 'hello' in this bubble!" and models drawing a wave symbol or a simple scribble for "hello." Students draw in their first bubble. Then, the teacher says, "Now, draw 'goodbye' in the other bubble!" and models a 'goodbye' symbol or scribble. Students follow.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice: Drawing Polite Actions.</b> Students receive a worksheet with two scenes: 1) a child looking at a toy, 2) a child happily receiving a toy. The teacher instructs, "In this box (pointing to scene 1), draw a picture or scribble that means 'please'! How do you ask for something politely?" (Teacher models a simple open hand drawing/scribble). Then, "In this box (pointing to scene 2), draw a picture or scribble that means 'thank you'! How do you show you are happy for a toy?" (Teacher models a simple gift drawing/scribble).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction/Peer Check: My Polite Friend Drawing.</b> Students are given a small blank paper. The teacher says, "Draw yourself and a friend saying 'hello'!" or "Draw yourself sharing a toy with a friend and showing 'please' or 'thank you'!" After drawing, students turn to a partner, show their drawing, and point to elements, using gestures or simple sounds to explain (e.g., pointing to a wave and saying "hello," pointing to a shared toy and showing "thank you"). The partner attempts to understand and react by pointing to their own drawing.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Meeting My Friends."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Task: "My Polite Story" Booklet.</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Individual written product (a series of 3 drawings on a pre-folded paper booklet or a strip divided into 3 squares).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b> The teacher provides each student with a paper strip divided into three sections. "Today, you will draw a little story about being polite with your friends!"</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Section 1:</b> "Draw yourself meeting a friend and saying 'hello'!" (Teacher models a simple drawing of two stick figures waving and a scribble for 'hello'). Students draw their representation.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Section 2:</b> "Now, draw yourself and your friend sharing a toy. Show someone saying 'please' and someone saying 'thank you'!" (Teacher models a simple drawing of two stick figures with a toy between them, and distinct scribbles/symbols for 'please' and 'thank you' in speech bubbles). Students draw their representation.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Section 3:</b> "Finally, draw yourself and your friend saying 'goodbye'!" (Teacher models two stick figures waving goodbye, with a scribble for 'goodbye', focusing on the /g/ sound). Students draw their representation.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">The teacher circulates, providing encouragement, asking "What are you drawing here?" to prompt verbalization, and offering simple drawing cues as needed. The focus is on the children's ability to communicate the concepts visually, even through scribbles.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy and student self-reflection.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the "My Polite Story" booklet activity.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each student's individual "My Polite Story" drawing strip/booklet.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Successfully attempted to draw/scribble "hello" (e.g., wave symbol, a scribble that they identify as "hello").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Successfully attempted to draw/scribble "please" (e.g., asking gesture, simple scribble).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Successfully attempted to draw/scribble "thank you" (e.g., giving gesture, simple scribble).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Shows effort to communicate a message through drawing/scribbling.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> The teacher holds up visual cues (e.g., a flashcard of two children saying "hello," another for "thank you").</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did you draw a friend saying 'hello' in your story?" (Students point to their drawing if yes).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Did you draw 'please' or 'thank you' in your story?" (Students point to the relevant part of their drawing if yes).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt (verbally/gesturally for Pre-K):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Which picture made you happy to draw today?" (Students point to their favorite drawing).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can you show me a picture where someone is being polite?" (Students point to the 'please' or 'thank you' drawing).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> Today, the students were enthusiastic about expressing ideas through drawing. They showed good recognition of "hello" and "thank you" in their scribbles, but representing "please" visually proved a bit more challenging for some. The sequence of drawing prompts in the "My Polite Story" helped them structure their ideas. Some students needed more individual support in associating specific scribbles with words. I will need to provide more explicit visual examples of simple symbols for "please" in future activities. Reinforcing initial /f/ in "friend" and /g/ in "goodbye" through short, focused repetition during modeling helped.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> In Lesson 5 (Mediation & Integration), students will consolidate their learning by using their individual drawings from today to contribute to a collaborative classroom "Friends' Polite Book." They will present their drawings to their peers, using simple oral language ("This is my friend, I say hello!") and gestures, demonstrating the concepts of saying "please" and "thank you" in a shared project.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3-5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>